{"metadata":{"kernelspec":{"language":"python","display_name":"Python 3","name":"python3"},"language_info":{"name":"python","version":"3.12.13","mimetype":"text/x-python","codemirror_mode":{"name":"ipython","version":3},"pygments_lexer":"ipython3","nbconvert_exporter":"python","file_extension":".py"},"kaggle":{"accelerator":"none","dataSources":[],"dockerImageVersionId":31400,"isInternetEnabled":true,"language":"python","sourceType":"notebook","isGpuEnabled":false}},"nbformat_minor":4,"nbformat":4,"cells":[{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"# Why We Need Statistics in Data Science\n\n*A hands-on walk-through, built from Dr. Abhinanda Sarkar's classroom session (Great Learning).*\n\nData science and machine learning have been called the hardest job of the 21st\ncentury. Whatever the title, one thing is constant underneath all of it:\n**statistics is the language you use to reason about data you cannot fully see.**\n\nWith statistics we make claims like *\"sales will fall most in this customer\nsegment next quarter\"* or *\"there's a 16% chance this positive HIV test is a true\npositive.\"* That isn't magic and it isn't a crystal ball - it's just statistics.\n\nThis notebook is a guided tour. Every idea is paired with **runnable code** so you\ncan change a number and watch the idea respond. The arc is:\n\n1. **Two ways of thinking** - statistics vs. machine learning\n2. **Descriptive → Predictive → Prescriptive** - the three jobs of analytics\n3. **Descriptive statistics** - mean vs. median, spread, skew, outliers\n4. **Correlation & covariance** - how two variables move together\n5. **Linear regression as description** - summarizing many variables at once\n6. **Probability** - coping with uncertainty\n7. **Conditional probability & Bayes' theorem** - spam filters and medical tests\n8. **Distributions** - binomial, Poisson, and the normal distribution\n\nRun the next cell first - it sets up everything.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"code","source":"import numpy as np\nimport pandas as pd\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\nfrom scipy import stats\n\nnp.random.seed(42)          # so the synthetic data is reproducible\nplt.rcParams['figure.figsize'] = (8, 4)\nplt.rcParams['axes.grid'] = True\nprint(\"Environment ready.\")","metadata":{"trusted":true,"execution":{"iopub.status.busy":"2026-06-04T11:58:48.893181Z","iopub.execute_input":"2026-06-04T11:58:48.893407Z","iopub.status.idle":"2026-06-04T11:58:52.208462Z","shell.execute_reply.started":"2026-06-04T11:58:48.893382Z","shell.execute_reply":"2026-06-04T11:58:52.207481Z"}},"outputs":[{"name":"stdout","text":"Environment ready.\n","output_type":"stream"}],"execution_count":1},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 1. Two ways of thinking: statistics vs. machine learning\n\nThere is a real cultural difference between the two communities:\n\n- The **statistical** way of thinking says: *first formulate a problem, then go\n  get the data to solve it.* Here **data is cheap and the question is expensive** -\n  you are paid for asking the right question.\n  \n- The **machine-learning** way of thinking says: *here is the data, tell me what it\n  is telling you.* Here **the question is cheap and the data is expensive** - you are\n  paid for collecting and exploiting the data.\n\nThe classic interview deadlock captures it:\n\n> **Business:** \"Here is my data. What can you say?\"\n> **Statistician:** \"What do you want to know?\"\n> **Business:** \"That's why I want to hire *you*.\"\n> **Statistician:** \"If you don't tell me what you want to know, how do I know what to tell you?\"\n\nNeither side is wrong. Real work lives in between. Take a concrete example: a watch\ncompany's sales are falling. You can't answer *\"why?\"* by staring at numbers - you\nhave to **frame questions first**: Which segments are declining? How fast? Where is\nthe bulk of sales? Only then does the data become useful.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 2. Descriptive, Predictive, Prescriptive\n\nAnalytics work usually flows through three stages. The cricket analogy makes them\nclick instantly:\n\n| Stage | Question | Cricket example |\n|---|---|---|\n| **Descriptive** | What happened? It locates and isolates the problem. | How many centuries has the batsman scored? (look it up) |\n| **Predictive** | What *will* happen, or what happens if I change something? | How many centuries will he score in the World Cup? |\n| **Prescriptive** | What should I *do* to change the outcome? | How do we get him to score *more* centuries? |\n\nA doctor does all three: *describe* your symptoms, *predict* you are becoming\npre-diabetic, then *prescribe* an action - under constraints (first, do no harm).\nA business does the same: sell more watches, but stay profitable, keep employees,\nrepay loans. Prescription is a constrained optimization problem.\n\n**This notebook focuses mostly on the descriptive foundation**, because everything\npredictive and prescriptive is built on top of it.","metadata":{}},{"cell_type":"markdown","source":"## 3. A dataset to think with: CardioGoodFitness\n\nThe market-research team of a fitness company wants the **profile of the typical\ncustomer** for each treadmill product. They collect data on people who bought a\ntreadmill in the last three months.\n\nWe generate a synthetic version of the well-known *CardioGoodFitness* dataset so\nevery cell here runs with no external file. The columns mirror the real study:\n\n- `Product` - model bought (TM195, TM498, TM798)\n- `Gender`, `Age`, `Education` (years),\n- `MaritalStatus`\n- `Income` - annual household income\n- `Usage` - times per week the customer plans to use it\n- `Fitness` - self-rated 1 (poor) to 5 (excellent)\n- `Miles` - expected miles walked/run per week\n\nNotice that some of these are **measured facts** (age, income) and some are\n**opinion masquerading as data** (planned usage, self-rated fitness). It's still\ndata - it came from somewhere.","metadata":{}}]}